<section id="scenario">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="head">
      <div>
        <p class="kicker">The scenario</p>
        <h2>One service morning. The equity alert actually reaches someone.</h2>
      </div>
      <p class="sub">Customers check in for service. Some have equity, an ending lease or an expiring warranty. Instead of landing on a dashboard nobody opens, each flag goes to the advisor on the write-up and a named salesperson, and the manager sees whether anyone acted.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="steps">
      <div class="step"><div class="n">VIEW 1</div><h3>OVERVIEW</h3><p>The morning at a glance: who's in the lane today and why some of them are flagged.</p></div>
      <div class="step"><div class="n">VIEW 2</div><h3>SERVICE LANE</h3><p>What the advisor sees. Flagged customers show the reason and a talking point that isn't a sales pitch.</p></div>
      <div class="step"><div class="n">VIEW 3</div><h3>SALESPERSON</h3><p>The alert arrives by text. The salesperson claims the customer and logs what happened.</p></div>
      <div class="step"><div class="n">VIEW 4</div><h3>MANAGER</h3><p>Unclaimed flags escalate after ten minutes. The dashboard counts flagged, contacted on site, appointments and sold.</p></div>
      <div class="step"><div class="n">THE POINT</div><h3>OWNED AND MEASURED</h3><p>Every flag has an owner, an outcome and a number behind it. That's the difference between an alert and a workflow.</p></div>
      <div class="step last"><div class="n">IN YOUR STORE</div><h3>YOUR TOOLS FIRST</h3><p>We implement the equity tool you already own before building anything. The demo shows the pattern, not a product you have to buy.</p></div>
    </div>
    <p class="deptnote">Sample data only. No real customers, vehicles or results.</p>
  </div>
</section>

